            "output": "     Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures - Illinois Experts             \n\n[Skip to main navigation](#main-navigation) [Skip to search](#main-search) [Skip to main content](#main-content)\n\n[Illinois Experts Home ![Illinois Experts Logo](/skin/headerImage/)](/en/) \n\n-   [LOGIN & Help   Link opens in a new tab](http://go.illinois.edu/experts)\n\nSearch content at Illinois Experts \n\n-   [Home](/en/)\n-   [Profiles](/en/persons/)\n-   [Research units](/en/organisations/)\n-   [Research & Scholarship](/en/publications/)\n-   [Datasets](/en/datasets/)\n-   [Honors](/en/prizes/)\n-   [Press/Media](/en/clippings/)\n-   [Activities](/en/activities/)\n\n# Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures\n\n-   Henrique R.A. Freitas\n-   , [Celso L. Mendes](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/)\n-   , Aleksandar Ilic\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\n\n[](https://plu.mx/plum/a/?doi=10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271)\n\n-   [Overview](/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/)\n-   [Fingerprint](/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/fingerprints/)\n\n## Abstract\n\nLarge-scale hydrological models simulate watershed processes with applications in water resources, climate change, land use, and forecast systems. The quality of the simulations mainly depends on calibrating optimal sets of watershed parameters, a time-consuming task that highly demands computational resources from repeated simulations. This work aims at performance optimizations on the MGB (“Modelo de Grandes Bacias”) hydrological model and the MOCOM-UA (Multi-Objective Complex Evolution) calibration method for two watersheds. The optimizations target state-of-the-art CPU/GPU systems, exploiting techniques that include AVX-512 vectorization, and multi-core (CPU) and many-core (GPU) parallelisms. Significant speedups of up to 20 × (CPU) were achieved for calibration, while the scalability analysis indicated 24 × (CPU) and 65 × (GPU) for simulations with larger problem sizes. The roofline analysis confirmed more effective use of the hardware resources, and the quantitative accuracy evaluation of the optimized implementations reached maximum relative errors of approximately 6% for discharges and objective functions.\n\nOriginal language\n\nEnglish (US)\n\nArticle number\n\n105271\n\nJournal\n\n[Environmental Modelling and Software](#)\n\nVolume\n\n148\n\nDOIs\n\n-   [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271)\n\nState\n\nPublished - Feb 2022\n\nExternally published\n\nYes\n\n## Keywords\n\n-   High performance computing\n-   Hydrology models\n-   Parallel processing\n-   Parameterization\n-   Roofline model\n-   Vectorization\n\n## ASJC Scopus subject areas\n\n-   Software\n-   Environmental Engineering\n-   Ecological Modeling\n\n## Online availability\n\n-   [10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271)\n    \n\n## Library availability\n\n[Discover UIUC Full Text](https://i-share-uiu.primo.exlibrisgroup.com/openurl/01CARLI_UIU/01CARLI_UIU:CARLI_UIU?ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&ctx_tim=2026-10-19T06%3A44%3A15UTC&ctx_enc=info%3Aofi%2FencUTF-8&url_ver=Z39.88-2004&url_ctx_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Actx&rft.genre=article&rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Fkev%3Afmt%3Ajournal&rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fpure.atira.dk%3Apure&rft.atitle=Performance%20optimization%20of%20the%20MGB%20hydrological%20model%20for%20multi-core%20and%20GPU%20architectures&rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1016%2Fj.envsoft.2021.105271&rft.aulast=Freitas&rft.aufirst=Henrique%20R.A.&rft.auinit=H&rft.date=2022-02&rft.volume=148&rft.issn=1364-8152&rft.jtitle=Environmental%20Modelling%20and%20Software)\n\n## Related links\n\n-   [Link to publication in Scopus](https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85121208432)\n    \n-   [Link to the citations in Scopus](https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85121208432#tab=citedBy)\n    \n\n## Fingerprint\n\nDive into the research topics of 'Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\n\n-   Multicore Architecture Keyphrases 100%\n    \n-   Performance Optimization Keyphrases 100%\n    \n-   Hydrological Model Keyphrases 100%\n    \n-   Multi-GPU Architectures Keyphrases 100%\n    \n-   Multicore Computer Science 100%\n    \n-   Graphics Processing Unit Computer Science 100%\n    \n-   Calibration Method Keyphrases 50%\n    \n-   Computational Resources Keyphrases 50%\n    \n\n[View full fingerprint](/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/fingerprints/)\n\n## Cite this\n\n-   APA\n-   Standard\n-   Harvard\n-   Vancouver\n-   Author\n-   BIBTEX\n-   RIS\n\nFreitas, H. R. A.[, Mendes, C. L.](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/), & Ilic, A. (2022). [Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/). *Environmental Modelling and Software*, *148*, Article 105271. [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271)\n\n[**Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures.**](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/) / Freitas, Henrique R.A.[; Mendes, Celso L.](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/); Ilic, Aleksandar.  \nIn: Environmental Modelling and Software, Vol. 148, 105271, 02.2022.\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\n\nFreitas, HRA[, Mendes, CL](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/) & Ilic, A 2022, '[Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/)', *Environmental Modelling and Software*, vol. 148, 105271. [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271)\n\nFreitas HRA[, Mendes CL](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/), Ilic A. [Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/). Environmental Modelling and Software. 2022 Feb;148:105271. doi: 10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271\n\nFreitas, Henrique R.A. [; Mendes, Celso L.](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/) ; Ilic, Aleksandar. / [**Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures**](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/). In: [Environmental Modelling and Software](#). 2022 ; Vol. 148.\n\n@article{19ce0bb9118545d6ad2a55bfe8d415ce,\n\ntitle = \"Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures\",\n\nabstract = \"Large-scale hydrological models simulate watershed processes with applications in water resources, climate change, land use, and forecast systems. The quality of the simulations mainly depends on calibrating optimal sets of watershed parameters, a time-consuming task that highly demands computational resources from repeated simulations. This work aims at performance optimizations on the MGB ({\\\\textquotedblleft}Modelo de Grandes Bacias{\\\\textquotedblright}) hydrological model and the MOCOM-UA (Multi-Objective Complex Evolution) calibration method for two watersheds. The optimizations target state-of-the-art CPU/GPU systems, exploiting techniques that include AVX-512 vectorization, and multi-core (CPU) and many-core (GPU) parallelisms. Significant speedups of up to 20 {\\\\texttimes} (CPU) were achieved for calibration, while the scalability analysis indicated 24 {\\\\texttimes} (CPU) and 65 {\\\\texttimes} (GPU) for simulations with larger problem sizes. The roofline analysis confirmed more effective use of the hardware resources, and the quantitative accuracy evaluation of the optimized implementations reached maximum relative errors of approximately 6\\\\% for discharges and objective functions.\",\n\nkeywords = \"High performance computing, Hydrology models, Parallel processing, Parameterization, Roofline model, Vectorization\",\n\nauthor = \"Freitas, \\\\{Henrique R.A.\\\\} and Mendes, \\\\{Celso L.\\\\} and Aleksandar Ilic\",\n\nnote = \"Modern CPUs support vector extensions up to 512 bits (current Intel CPUs) for Single Instruction Multiple Data (SIMD) data-parallel strategies. Compilers are not fully effective in automatic vectorization, so hand-tuned explicitly vectorized codes execute faster than the auto-vectorized equivalents. However, explicit vectorization requires careful and detailed code analysis (low-level instructions), and more development time (Mitra et al., 2013). The additional availability of multiple cores in CPUs (Ralston, 2008) also enables thread-parallel strategies in shared-memory environments. The workload is distributed to multiple CPU cores, identified as threads, and processed in parallel. The OpenMP standard (Dagum and Menon, 1998) executes applications with thread-level parallelism via compiler directives that dynamically activate/deactivate parallel threads.The STE, DIS, and CON routines were executed on GPU as CUDA kernels with the CUDA Fortran interface (NVIDIA, 2020a), supported by the PGI pgf90 compiler (PGI, 2020). The CUDA framework was chosen because we previously applied directive-based GPU programming standards (OpenACC) on the MGB model (Freitas and Mendes, 2019), but that particular optimization decreased the performance, mainly caused by overheads associated to the OpenACC directives that increased runtimes from millions of routine calls, not amortized by thousands of loop iterations (catchments), i.e. computations were not GPU intensive.The authors are grateful to Walter Collischonn from IPH-UFRGS for the availability of the MGB hydrological model and datasets. This work was financially supported by the Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Cient{\\\\'i}fico e Tecnol{\\\\'o}gico (CNPq), Brazil, grant number 140 687/2 016-5; and by Portuguese national funds through Funda{\\\\c c}{\\\\~a}o para a Ci{\\\\^e}ncia e a Tecnologia (FCT), under projects UIDB/50 021/2 020 and PTDC/CCI-COM/31 901/2 017. The authors are grateful to Walter Collischonn from IPH-UFRGS for the availability of the MGB hydrological model and datasets. This work was financially supported by the Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Cient{\\\\'i}fico e Tecnol{\\\\'o}gico (CNPq) , Brazil, grant number 140 687/2 016-5; and by Portuguese national funds through Funda{\\\\c c}{\\\\~a}o para a Ci{\\\\^e}ncia e a Tecnologia (FCT) , under projects UIDB/50 021/2 020 and PTDC/CCI-COM/31 901/2 017.\",\n\nyear = \"2022\",\n\nmonth = feb,\n\ndoi = \"10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271\",\n\nlanguage = \"English (US)\",\n\nvolume = \"148\",\n\njournal = \"Environmental Modelling and Software\",\n\nissn = \"1364-8152\",\n\npublisher = \"Elsevier Ltd\",\n\n}\n\nTY - JOUR\n\nT1 - Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures\n\nAU - Freitas, Henrique R.A.\n\nAU - Mendes, Celso L.\n\nAU - Ilic, Aleksandar\n\nN1 - Modern CPUs support vector extensions up to 512 bits (current Intel CPUs) for Single Instruction Multiple Data (SIMD) data-parallel strategies. Compilers are not fully effective in automatic vectorization, so hand-tuned explicitly vectorized codes execute faster than the auto-vectorized equivalents. However, explicit vectorization requires careful and detailed code analysis (low-level instructions), and more development time (Mitra et al., 2013). The additional availability of multiple cores in CPUs (Ralston, 2008) also enables thread-parallel strategies in shared-memory environments. The workload is distributed to multiple CPU cores, identified as threads, and processed in parallel. The OpenMP standard (Dagum and Menon, 1998) executes applications with thread-level parallelism via compiler directives that dynamically activate/deactivate parallel threads.The STE, DIS, and CON routines were executed on GPU as CUDA kernels with the CUDA Fortran interface (NVIDIA, 2020a), supported by the PGI pgf90 compiler (PGI, 2020). The CUDA framework was chosen because we previously applied directive-based GPU programming standards (OpenACC) on the MGB model (Freitas and Mendes, 2019), but that particular optimization decreased the performance, mainly caused by overheads associated to the OpenACC directives that increased runtimes from millions of routine calls, not amortized by thousands of loop iterations (catchments), i.e. computations were not GPU intensive.The authors are grateful to Walter Collischonn from IPH-UFRGS for the availability of the MGB hydrological model and datasets. This work was financially supported by the Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico (CNPq), Brazil, grant number 140 687/2 016-5; and by Portuguese national funds through Fundação para a Ciência e a Tecnologia (FCT), under projects UIDB/50 021/2 020 and PTDC/CCI-COM/31 901/2 017. The authors are grateful to Walter Collischonn from IPH-UFRGS for the availability of the MGB hydrological model and datasets. This work was financially supported by the Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico (CNPq) , Brazil, grant number 140 687/2 016-5; and by Portuguese national funds through Fundação para a Ciência e a Tecnologia (FCT) , under projects UIDB/50 021/2 020 and PTDC/CCI-COM/31 901/2 017.\n\nPY - 2022/2\n\nY1 - 2022/2\n\nN2 - Large-scale hydrological models simulate watershed processes with applications in water resources, climate change, land use, and forecast systems. The quality of the simulations mainly depends on calibrating optimal sets of watershed parameters, a time-consuming task that highly demands computational resources from repeated simulations. This work aims at performance optimizations on the MGB (“Modelo de Grandes Bacias”) hydrological model and the MOCOM-UA (Multi-Objective Complex Evolution) calibration method for two watersheds. The optimizations target state-of-the-art CPU/GPU systems, exploiting techniques that include AVX-512 vectorization, and multi-core (CPU) and many-core (GPU) parallelisms. Significant speedups of up to 20 × (CPU) were achieved for calibration, while the scalability analysis indicated 24 × (CPU) and 65 × (GPU) for simulations with larger problem sizes. The roofline analysis confirmed more effective use of the hardware resources, and the quantitative accuracy evaluation of the optimized implementations reached maximum relative errors of approximately 6% for discharges and objective functions.\n\nAB - Large-scale hydrological models simulate watershed processes with applications in water resources, climate change, land use, and forecast systems. The quality of the simulations mainly depends on calibrating optimal sets of watershed parameters, a time-consuming task that highly demands computational resources from repeated simulations. This work aims at performance optimizations on the MGB (“Modelo de Grandes Bacias”) hydrological model and the MOCOM-UA (Multi-Objective Complex Evolution) calibration method for two watersheds. The optimizations target state-of-the-art CPU/GPU systems, exploiting techniques that include AVX-512 vectorization, and multi-core (CPU) and many-core (GPU) parallelisms. Significant speedups of up to 20 × (CPU) were achieved for calibration, while the scalability analysis indicated 24 × (CPU) and 65 × (GPU) for simulations with larger problem sizes. The roofline analysis confirmed more effective use of the hardware resources, and the quantitative accuracy evaluation of the optimized implementations reached maximum relative errors of approximately 6% for discharges and objective functions.\n\nKW - High performance computing\n\nKW - Hydrology models\n\nKW - Parallel processing\n\nKW - Parameterization\n\nKW - Roofline model\n\nKW - Vectorization\n\nUR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85121208432\n\nUR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85121208432#tab=citedBy\n\nU2 - 10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271\n\nDO - 10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271\n\nM3 - Article\n\nAN - SCOPUS:85121208432\n\nSN - 1364-8152\n\nVL - 148\n\nJO - Environmental Modelling and Software\n\nJF - Environmental Modelling and Software\n\nM1 - 105271\n\nER -\n\n-   [](https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/%3Futm_source%3Dfacebook%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dsharelink%26doi%3D10.1016%2Fj.envsoft.2021.105271&p[summary]=Check+out+this+research+output+at+Illinois+Experts%3A+Performance+optimization+of+the+MGB+hydrological+model+for+multi-core+and+GPU+architectures)\n-   [](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?url=https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/%3Futm_source%3Dtwitter%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dsharelink%26doi%3D10.1016%2Fj.envsoft.2021.105271&text=Check+out+this+research+output+at+Illinois+Experts%3A+Performance+optimization+of+the+MGB+hydrological+model+for+multi-core+and+GPU+architectures)\n-   [](https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?mini=true&url=https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/%3Futm_source%3Dlinkedin%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dsharelink%26doi%3D10.1016%2Fj.e