 your search could not be completed due to server timeouts. Please try again later.\n\n# Empty search term\n\nYou have applied the search with an empty search term. Please revisit and try again.\n\n# Too many requests\n\nWe are sorry, but we have received too many parallel search requests. Please try again later.\n\nSearching\n\n \n\n[Articles](https://hess.copernicus.org/) | [Volume 21, issue 10](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/issue10.html)\n\n-   [Article](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.html)\n-   [Assets](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-assets.html)\n-   [Peer review](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-discussion.html)\n-   [Metrics](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-metrics.html)\n-   [Related articles](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-relations.html)\n\n[Articles](https://hess.copernicus.org/) | [Volume 21, issue 10](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/issue10.html)\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.5194/hess-21-5293-2017\n\n© Author(s) 2017. This work is distributed under  \nthe Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 License.\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.5194/hess-21-5293-2017\n\n© Author(s) 2017. This work is distributed under  \nthe Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 License.\n\n[Articles](https://hess.copernicus.org/) | [Volume 21, issue 10](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/issue10.html)\n\n-   [Article](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.html)\n-   [Assets](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-assets.html)\n-   [Peer review](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-discussion.html)\n-   [Metrics](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-metrics.html)\n-   [Related articles](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-relations.html)\n\nResearch article\n\n | **Highlight paper**\n\n | [![](https://contentmanager.copernicus.org/2175966/13/ssl)](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/)\n\n20 Oct 2017\n\nResearch article | **Highlight paper** | [![](https://contentmanager.copernicus.org/2175966/13/ssl)](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/) | 20 Oct 2017\n\n[![](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-avatar-thumb150.png)](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-avatar-web.png)\n\n# The CAMELS data set: catchment attributes and meteorology for large-sample studies\n\nNans Addor[](mailto:n.addor@uea.ac.uk), Andrew J. Newman, Naoki Mizukami, and Martyn P. Clark\n\n### Nans Addor\n\nCORRESPONDING AUTHOR\n\n[n.addor@uea.ac.uk](mailto:n.addor@uea.ac.uk)\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6057-3930](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6057-3930)\n\n×\n\nResearch Applications Laboratory, National Center for Atmospheric Research, Boulder, CO, USA\n\nnow at: Climatic Research Unit, School of Environmental Sciences, University of East Anglia, Norwich, UK\n\n### Andrew J. Newman\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8796-0861](https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8796-0861)\n\n×\n\nResearch Applications Laboratory, National Center for Atmospheric Research, Boulder, CO, USA\n\n### Naoki Mizukami\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0893-5869](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0893-5869)\n\n×\n\nResearch Applications Laboratory, National Center for Atmospheric Research, Boulder, CO, USA\n\n### Martyn P. Clark\n\n×\n\nResearch Applications Laboratory, National Center for Atmospheric Research, Boulder, CO, USA\n\n**Abstract.** We present a new data set of attributes for 671 catchments in the contiguous United States (CONUS) minimally impacted by human activities. This complements the daily time series of meteorological forcing and streamflow provided by Newman et al. (2015b). To produce this extension, we synthesized diverse and complementary data sets to describe six main classes of attributes at the catchment scale: topography, climate, streamflow, land cover, soil, and geology. The spatial variations among basins over the CONUS are discussed and compared using a series of maps. The large number of catchments, combined with the diversity of the attributes we extracted, makes this new data set well suited for large-sample studies and comparative hydrology. In comparison to the similar Model Parameter Estimation Experiment (MOPEX) data set, this data set relies on more recent data, it covers a wider range of attributes, and its catchments are more evenly distributed across the CONUS. This study also involves assessments of the limitations of the source data sets used to compute catchment attributes, as well as detailed descriptions of how the attributes were computed. The hydrometeorological time series provided by Newman et al. (2015b, [https://doi.org/10.5065/D6MW2F4D](https://doi.org/10.5065/D6MW2F4D)) together with the catchment attributes introduced in this paper ([https://doi.org/10.5065/D6G73C3Q](https://doi.org/10.5065/D6G73C3Q)) constitute the freely available CAMELS data set, which stands for Catchment Attributes and MEteorology for Large-sample Studies.\n\nDownload & links\n\n-   [Article (PDF, 8761 KB)](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.pdf \"PDF Version (8761 KB)\")\n\nDownload & links\n\n-   [Article](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.pdf \"PDF Version (8761 KB)\") (8761 KB)\n-   [Full-text XML](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.xml \"XML Version\")\n-   [BibTeX](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.bib)\n-   [EndNote](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.ris)\n\nShare\n\n[![Mendeley](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/mendeley.png)](https://www.mendeley.com/import/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Mendeley\")\n\n[![Reddit](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/reddit.png)](https://www.reddit.com/submit?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Reddit\")\n\n[![Twitter](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/twitter.png)](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Twitter\")\n\n[![Facebook](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/facebook.png)](https://www.facebook.com/share.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F&t=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies \"Facebook\")\n\n[![LinkedIn](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/linkedin.png)](https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?mini=true&url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F&title=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies \"LinkedIn\")\n\n[](#)\n\n**How to cite.** Addor, N., Newman, A. J., Mizukami, N., and Clark, M. P.: The CAMELS data set: catchment attributes and meteorology for large-sample studies, Hydrol. Earth Syst. Sci., 21, 5293–5313, https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-21-5293-2017, 2017.\n\nReceived: 22 Mar 2017 – Discussion started: 30 Mar 2017 – Revised: 04 Sep 2017 – Accepted: 08 Sep 2017 – Published: 20 Oct 2017\n\nDownload\n\n-   [Article](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.pdf \"PDF Version (8761 KB)\") (8761 KB)\n-   [Full-text XML](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.xml \"XML Version\")\n\n-   [BibTeX](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.bib)\n-   [EndNote](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.ris)\n\nShort summary\n\nWe introduce a data set describing the landscape of 671 catchments in the contiguous USA: we synthesized various data sources to characterize the topography, climate, streamflow, land cover, soil, and geology of each catchment. This extends the daily time series of meteorological forcing and discharge provided by an earlier study. The diversity of these catchments will help to improve our understanding and modeling of how the interplay between catchment attributes shapes hydrological processes.\n\nWe introduce a data set describing the landscape of 671 catchments in the contiguous USA: we...\n\n[Read more](#)\n\nShare\n\n[![Mendeley](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/mendeley.png)](https://www.mendeley.com/import/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Mendeley\")\n\n[![Reddit](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/reddit.png)](https://www.reddit.com/submit?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Reddit\")\n\n[![Twitter](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/twitter.png)](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Twitter\")\n\n[![Facebook](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/facebook.png)](https://www.facebook.com/share.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F&t=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies \"Facebook\")\n\n[![LinkedIn](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/linkedin.png)](https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?mini=true&url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F&title=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies \"LinkedIn\")\n\n[](#)\n\nHydrology and Earth System Sciences\n\nAn interactive open-access journal of the European Geosciences Union\n\n[![](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/graphic_egu_logo_white.png)](http: